--- Finding: input[] at the end of standard input answers empty text, ok,
--- again and again, so a program cannot tell the end of its input from a
--- blank line, and the natural loop that reads every line never ends.
---
--- Run with one line of input:   printf 'one\n' | crow run -i this.co
--- Interpreted and compiled agree (both wrong):
---     0: ok true, [one]
---     1: ok true, []
---     2: ok true, []
---     3: ok true, []
---     stopped with ok true after 4 reads
--- Expected: the read past the last line fails (an error value, .ok false,
--- as the spec's Fallible Builtins table says input[] does when "Stdin
--- read fails", and as Python's input() raises EOFError), so the loop ends
--- after one read:
---     0: ok true, [one]
---     stopped with ok false after 1 reads
--- Without the `n lt 4` guard (`while line.ok`), both modes loop forever:
--- a Rosetta solution reading its test strings this way wrote 390 MB of
--- `"" -> true` lines in 120 s before it was killed.
--- Cause (read, not changed): crow_runtime/src/core.rs `read_line` maps
--- `stdin().read_line` returning Ok(0) — end of input — to Ok("").
n = 0
line = input[]
while line.ok and n lt 4
    log['`n`: ok `line.ok`, [`line`]']
    n = n + 1
    line = input[]
log['stopped with ok `line.ok` after `n` reads']
